# 5. Clarify Directed Advection vs Undirected Diffusion in Section 3.1
old_diff = r"""Prior GNCAs (e.g., LGCA~\cite{grattarola2021learning}, $E(n)$-GNCAs~\cite{petric2023cellular}) operate on undirected geometric lattices using symmetric degree-scaled convolutions ($D^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}} A D^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}$). However, agent interaction traces are causal directed acyclic graphs (User $\to$ Tool $\to$ Arg $\to$ State $\to$ Return). We formulate asymmetric directed random-walk Laplacian filtering ($(A - I) = -L_{\text{rw}}$, where the random-walk Laplacian is defined as $L_{\text{rw}} = I - A$), where negative eigenvalues explicitly capture directed temporal drift and back-propagation of execution status rather than isotropic geometric smoothing."""

new_diff = r"""Prior GNCAs (e.g., LGCA~\cite{grattarola2021learning}, $E(n)$-GNCAs~\cite{petric2023cellular}) operate on undirected geometric lattices using symmetric degree-scaled convolutions ($D^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}} A D^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}$). In graph signal processing, symmetric Laplacians model isotropic diffusion (analogous to physical heat dispersion), which diffuses signals symmetrically in all directions. On causal execution traces, however, isotropic diffusion is catastrophic: it allows unobserved future execution steps to back-contaminate earlier intent nodes, violating the causal Markov boundary. We formulate asymmetric directed random-walk Laplacian filtering ($(A - I) = -L_{\text{rw}}$, where $L_{\text{rw}} = I - A$), where the non-symmetric drift operator models directed advection/convection, enforcing strict backward-only propagation of execution feedback along causal DAG edges ($r_{t-k} \to u_{t-k} \to \mathbf{w}_{\text{env}} \to \mathbf{g}_{\text{intent}}$) while completely blocking forward contamination."""

if old_diff in text:
    text = text.replace(old_diff, new_diff)
    print("Directed advection explanation successfully updated!")
else:
    print("WARNING: old_diff not found!")

